in recent times there have been massive  investments by companies and firms in  internal databases and this has provided  us the means to provide an unprecedented  look at the behavior of employees the  interaction of those employees with the  firm's customers and a new way to think  about and quantify employee productivity  so my research is about taking these  very large big data sets on the internal  organization of firms to better  understand employee productivity to  better understand the design of  incentive systems and to better  understand how these two aspects and we  brought together to improve the  interaction of a company with its  customers there are two kinds of data  that we are talking about here one is on  the internal behavior of firms of the  type routinely collected by human  resources management systems or of the  type collected routinely by ERP systems  or enterprise resource planning software  which collects data on what your  employees are doing how they're spending  time on the activities and combining  this with compensation data for example  the salaries quotas commissions putting  these two aspects together we can ask  which salaries work which quotas work  which bonuses work for which sales agent  and ask the more broader question should  we have bonuses at all do quotas work  well  kotas and bonuses and commissions have  very good incentive properties because  they give you a lot of incentives to put  in a lot of effort because they form a  goal that you have to work towards in  order to earn commissions while that is  true the flipside is many people  understand that quotas and things like  these also have some bad properties  related to gaming or timing issues if  salespeople are gaming the quota in this  manner it can actually create some  really bad behavior and distortions in  the past in theory it was thought that  these distortions are interesting but  intellectually a curious may be not that  important in the real world some of the  insights that we have been able to  generate using real data from real  companies is that the magnitudes of  these distortions can be so huge that  the positive effects of quotas and  bonuses may well be overwhelmed if they  are not designed correctly because these  distortions just have a huge magnitude  different people are different there is  a lot of heterogeneity among salespeople  amongst your employees and it's not  likely to be the case that there is a  one-size-fits-all approach the value of  the data is that we can actually measure  heterogeneity in employees employee  skill and we can look at the data and  say that this employee in this  particular territory for every time we  called on a particular doctor his  conversion of a call into sales volume  is very high while some other employee  when he called on the doctor for a same  number of calls his conversion or  production of sales is very low so we  have the ability to use the data to  fine-tune contracts to the individual  employee level for example certain kinds  of employees who are quote unquote  high-powered might prefer that most of  their compensation is coming from  Commission's as opposed to straight  salary so if you hire the right kind of  employees you could make a commission  basis  work much better than if you have  employees who prefer straight salary on  the other hand if you are forced because  of institutional or within company  considerations that you can have only a  particular kind of incentive scheme you  might think well I need to hire the  right kind of employees in order to make  this incentive scheme work what I'm  trying to say here is that the hiring  and firing of employees as well as the  design of incentives to incent them have  to go hand in hand
 

